                  "endLine": 98,
                  "startLine": 98
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "message": {
            "text": "A logging call directly references a credential-named variable or field. Log processors and retention can broaden access to secrets. Remediation: Log nonsecret identifiers and outcomes; redact credentials before formatting and restrict log access and retention. Agent/MCP relevance: Agent traces and MCP logs commonly cross observability vendors, support systems and retention tiers. Logging a credential widens who can use the underlying model or tool authority beyond its intended worker. Fix steps: 1. Replace logged secret variables with request IDs, credential fingerprints suitable for the system, or nonsecret status. Redact at the source before string formatting and serialization. Verify: Run success/error paths with a synthetic credential marker and confirm it is absent from local logs, structured fields and exported traces. 2. Rotate actual exposed credentials and locate affected log copies, dashboards and exports. Restrict access and apply the applicable retention/deletion policy. Verify: Verify the old credential is invalid and unauthorized users cannot query affected log records; retain incident evidence in a protected form. 3. Add secret-safe logging helpers and tests for request headers, nested dictionaries and exception objects; avoid logging entire model/client configurations. Verify: Exercise nested credential fields and failures in the deployed collector path and confirm redaction happens before external transmission."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "agentMcpScan/v1": "4c41f364f09aeb4440e11ab1"
          },
          "properties": {
